  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We are given a step function on 85 points that achieves an overlap integral of approximately 0.38085. Our goal is to find a function with a lower integral by optimizing the function values subject to constraints. We use a constrained optimization approach with local search using the SLSQP method from SciPy, which can handle both box constraints and equality constraints. This method is chosen because it efficiently handles the bounds (h_i in [0,1]) and the equality constraint (sum(h) = 85/2). The time budget is respected by using a parameter limiting the maximum number of iterations per optimization run.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Domain: [0,2] discretized into 85 points\n   - Variables: h_i for i \u2208 [0, 84], where h_i \u2208 [0,1]\n   - Equality constraint: sum(h) = 85/2\n   - Objective function: \n     f(h) = max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')   [since dx is constant, it is equivalent to minimize the integral]\n   - Variables transformation: None needed as the variables are already in the required form.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Define helper functions:\n     score(h): Computes the overlap integral as dx * max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n     feasible_point(h): Checks that 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and sum(h) == 85/2\n   - Input validation in the objective function\n   - Objective function for optimizer: omits the dx factor to simplify optimization\n   - Initial point: uses the provided initial_h_values\n   - Constraints: box constraints [0,1] for each h_i and equality constraint sum(h)=85/2\n   - Optimization method: scipy.optimize.minimize with SLSQP algorithm\n   - Time tracking: uses timeit.default_timer() to check against budget\n   - Optimization options: iterations limited to 10000 to ensure timely termination\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - We use a single local optimization from the initial point due to time constraints\n   - No random restarts to keep implementation simple\n   - The SLSQP method with analytic derivatives provided via SciPy will handle the constraints\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Estimated runtime per optimization: around 5-10 seconds per run\n   - With time budget 1300 seconds, allows about 100-200 optimization runs if time remains\n   - Uses exact formulation with correlations computed as per problem requirements\n   - Handles potential evaluation failures by skipping erroneous points\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Check objective function value is finite\n   - Verify constraints satisfied after optimization\n   - Compute final score using the provided correlation method\n   - Return a tuple (h_values, score, n_points) for the best found solution\n   - Handles 85-point grid exactly, evaluation uses mode='full' correlation",
